<TopicPage slug="subjects/engineering/intermediate/fluid-mechanics">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Fluid mechanics for engineers is the <strong>physics lesson made measurable and
    money-relevant</strong>: pipes sized by pressure drop, pumps chosen from performance curves,
    and Reynolds number deciding what regime every flow is in. Nearly every engineered system —
    water, air, fuel, blood-loop devices — is a fluid circuit, and the fluid circuit has a
    particular engineering character: the governing equations (Navier–Stokes) have no general
    solution, so practice runs on a small set of exact results, a book of empirical
    correlations, and dimensionless numbers that let one test stand in for thousands. This
    page walks that toolkit from statics to pumps.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="boundary layers, Reynolds, pipe economics, drag" widget="equationStep" config={fluidEngCfg} />

  <h2>Fluid properties: viscosity, and fluids that misbehave</h2>
  <p>
    A fluid deforms continuously under shear, and <strong>viscosity</strong> (μ) is its
    resistance: the shear stress needed to slide one layer over another. Water and air are
    <strong>Newtonian</strong> — stress proportional to shear rate, viscosity constant at a
    given temperature (water: 0.001 Pa·s at 20 °C; air: about 0.000018). Everything with
    structure is <strong>non-Newtonian</strong>: blood (shear-thinning — red cells align and
    it flows easier at speed), paint and toothpaste (shear-thinning too, which is why they
    spread under a brush and stay on the wall), ketchup (yield stress — it refuses to move
    until you push hard enough, hence smacking the bottle), and silly putty (viscoelastic —
    bounces fast, flows slowly). The practical consequence: a pipeline design for water
    cannot be reused for sludge or crude oil at low temperature — crude can behave as a
    Bingham plastic that needs a starting pressure just to unstick.
  </p>
  <p>
    One non-obvious property rule shapes everything else: fluids stick to walls. Velocity is
    exactly zero <em>at</em> the surface, rising to free-stream across the <strong>boundary
    layer</strong> — a zone millimetres to centimetres thick that carries essentially all the
    friction drag, most of the heat transfer, and (if it separates from the surface) the
    stall of wings and the drag of blunt bodies. Engineering fluid mechanics is very often
    boundary-layer management.
  </p>

  <h2>Fluid statics: pressure, depth and dams</h2>
  <p>
    In a still fluid, pressure grows with depth by p = p<sub>0</sub> + ρgh, equally in all
    directions (Pascal). Worked example: a water tower whose free surface stands 30 m above
    the tap delivers p = ρgh = 1000 × 9.81 × 30 ≈ 294 kPa — about 3 bar, the historic way
    small towns ran mains pressure without a pump in sight. A diver at 10 m feels one
    extra atmosphere (98 kPa) for exactly the same reason. The consequence for structures:
    hydrostatic load on a dam grows as depth² (average pressure half the bottom value,
    times the area), which is why dams are thick at the base — gravity-arch geometry is
    the load triangle drawn in concrete. Buoyancy (Archimedes: upthrust = weight of
    displaced fluid) closes the statics chapter, from ships (steel floats because it
    displaces air-cross-sectioned volume) to ballast in submarines.
  </p>

  <h2>Reynolds number: one number, two universes</h2>
  <p>
    The ratio of inertial to viscous forces, <strong>Re = ρvL/μ</strong> (v = speed, L = a
    characteristic length), tells you which flow universe you are in. Low Re — viscosity
    wins: smooth, layered <strong>laminar</strong> flow, velocity in neat profiles, mixing
    only by diffusion. High Re — inertia wins: <strong>turbulent</strong> flow, chaotic
    eddies, vigorous mixing, and friction that no longer behaves linearly. In a pipe,
    transition sits near Re ≈ 2,300; fully developed turbulence is established by ~4,000.
    Worked example: water at 2 m/s in a 100 mm pipe: Re = 1000 × 2 × 0.1 ⁄ 0.001 =
    200,000 — violently turbulent, like virtually every industrial pipe. Blood in a
    large artery: Re ≈ 1,000–4,000, laminar with marginal margins — which is why
    <em>heart murmurs</em> (turbulence from narrowed valves) are audible at all. The
    dimensionless trick is the real gift: two flows with the same Re behave the same way
    scaled, which is why a 1:50 model of a spillway in a laboratory flume can certify the
    full-size structure — and why the Froude number (inertia/gravity, V/√(gL)) governs
    free-surface models and the Mach number (speed/speed of sound, ~343 m/s at sea level)
    governs compressibility: match the right dimensionless number and the physics transfers.
  </p>

  <h2>Bernoulli: energy conservation with fine print</h2>
  <p>
    Bernoulli's equation — p + ½ρv² + ρgh = constant along a streamline — is energy
    conservation per unit volume for steady, incompressible, frictionless flow. It powers
    the <strong>venturi</strong> (constrict a pipe: continuity forces v up — A₁v₁ = A₂v₂ —
    and Bernoulli drops the pressure: carburettors, atomisers, spray bottles) and the
    <strong>pitot tube</strong> (ram air to a stop: Δp = ½ρv², so v = √(2Δp/ρ); a 500 Pa
    reading in air gives √(2 × 500 ⁄ 1.2) ≈ 29 m/s ≈ 104 km/h — the aircraft airspeed
    indicator, and the instrument whose icing contributed to Air France 447's loss in
    2009). The fine print matters: friction, pumps and height changes each add terms, and
    the full <strong>energy equation</strong> with a head-loss term is what pipe analysis
    actually uses.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "faster flow has lower pressure because the fluid
  wants to go faster." Causality runs the other way: a pressure difference (or a
  constriction) accelerates the fluid, and the pressure drop is the energy that paid for
  the speed-up. Bernoulli is a bookkeeping identity along a streamline — it explains the
  curtain sucked in at an open window and the spray of a perfume atomiser, but the
  "equal transit time" story for aircraft lift is simply false (aircraft fly fine with
  air arriving early on top); lift is a turning of the flow with pressure differences
  integrated over the surface.</p>

  <h2>Pipes: the arithmetic that pays the bills</h2>
  <p>
    Friction in turbulent pipe flow is captured by <strong>Darcy–Weisbach</strong>:
    Δp = f·(L/D)·(ρv²/2), with the friction factor f read from the Moody chart as a
    function of Re and relative roughness — Colebrook's implicit formula behind it, the
    last slide rule generation's daily bread. Worked example: our 2 m/s, 100 mm, 100 m
    long steel pipe: f ≈ 0.018 (Re = 200,000, commercial steel roughness) → Δp =
    0.018 × 1000 × (1000 × 4 ⁄ 2)... numerically: (ρv²/2) = 1000 × 4 ⁄ 2 = 2,000 Pa;
    times L/D = 1,000, times f = 0.018 → <strong>36 kPa</strong>. The pump pushing that
    water spends Q·Δp = (2 × 0.00785 m²) × 36,000 ≈ <strong>565 W per 100 m</strong> —
    about half a kilowatt of pure tax for friction. Quadruple it to 24/7 operation and
    one pipe run costs ~5 MWh a year: this is why a pipe one size bigger (halving
    velocity, quartering the loss) routinely pays for its extra steel in saved
    electricity — the capex/opex trade, drawn hydraulically. Transients add their own
    rules: slam a valve shut and the momentum has to go somewhere — water hammer
    (Δp ≈ ρ·c·Δv, with c ≈ 1,400 m/s in water-filled steel pipe) can burst mains, which
    is why valves close on schedules and surge tanks exist.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> doubling pipe diameter doubles capacity. It multiplies
  it by roughly 5–6 in turbulent flow: area grows as D² and velocity for the same
  pressure gradient grows roughly as D<sup>5/2</sup> through the friction law. Diameter
  is the pipe's exponent-laden superpower — and the reason undersizing a main is a
  decades-long regret.</p>

  <h2>Measuring flow, and channels that flow free</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Flow measurement</strong> is Bernoulli industrialised: <strong>venturi
    meters</strong> and orifice plates read the pressure drop across a constriction
    (cheap, standard, with a permanent pressure penalty for the orifice); <strong>pitot
    tubes</strong> read a point velocity; <strong>weirs</strong> on open channels read
    depth over a crest, depth mapping to discharge by a calibrated power law.
    <strong>Open-channel flow</strong> — rivers, canals, sewers — has a free surface and
    answers to gravity instead of pressure: Manning's equation, V = (1/n)·R<sup>2/3</sup>·S<sup>1/2</sup>
    (n = roughness, R = hydraulic radius, S = slope) is the workhorse; a canal's "n" is
    looked up from concrete (0.012) to weedy natural channel (0.035). Where fast shallow
    flow meets slow deep flow, the <strong>hydraulic jump</strong> — the standing wave
    below a weir — dissipates energy violently; spillway stilling basins build jumps
    <em>on purpose</em> so the flood cannot dig out the dam's toe. Ship designers know the
    Froude number as the wave-making tax: hull speed scales with √L, the reason long
    boats are fast boats.
  </p>

  <h2>Pumps, fans, turbines: machines on curves</h2>
  <p>
    A centrifugal <strong>pump</strong> has a characteristic curve: head delivered falls
    as flow rises. The <strong>system curve</strong> (static lift + friction ∝ v²) rises
    with flow. The <strong>operating point</strong> is their intersection — no controller
    puts it there; the physics does. The <strong>affinity laws</strong> scale a pump with
    speed: flow ∝ N, head ∝ N², power ∝ N³ — so trimming speed 20% cuts power to
    roughly 58% (1.2³ ≈ 1.73, so 1/1.73), the arithmetic behind variable-speed drives
    and why throttling with valves instead is thermodynamically silly. The pump's mortal
    enemy is <strong>cavitation</strong>: if suction-side pressure falls near the liquid's
    vapour pressure, bubbles form and collapse against the impeller with micro-jets that
    pit metal — sounds like gravel running through the pump, shortens its life to months.
    The <strong>NPSH</strong> calculation (available suction head above vapour pressure,
    vs the manufacturer's required value) is the reflex: lower the pump, shorten and
    enlarge the suction line, cool the liquid. <strong>Turbines</strong> are the same
    machines run backwards (impulse — Pelton jets — and reaction — Francis, Kaplan),
    compressors and fans the same logic on gases, and multi-pump installations combine
    the curves: <em>series</em> adds heads (same flow), <em>parallel</em> adds flows
    (same head) — with the caveat that parallel pumps can push each other off their
    best-efficiency point.
  </p>

  <h2>Gases, compressibility and the z factor</h2>
  <p>
    Liquids are incompressible in practice (a 1% squeeze needs ~200 bar); gases are not.
    The <strong>ideal gas law</strong> (pv = RT) covers them well at ordinary conditions;
    the correction for reality is the compressibility factor <strong>z</strong>: pv = zRT,
    with z ≈ 1 far from condensation, dipping well below (attractive forces dominate) and
    rising above 1 near the critical point (repulsion dominates) — at 300 K and 100 bar,
    methane's z is roughly 0.85, an 15% bookkeeping error if ignored, which is why gas
    pipeline and storage calculations carry z charts (or real-gas equations of state) as
    standard equipment. When speeds approach Mach 1 (343 m/s at sea level), compressibility
    stops being bookkeeping and becomes the design: nozzles, shock waves, and the reason
    commercial jets cruise at Mach 0.85 and not 0.99.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Turbulence: the unfinished physics.</strong> The Navier–Stokes equations are
    known; their turbulent solutions are not — existence and smoothness of the 3-D
    solutions is a Clay Millennium Prize, and turbulence itself remains the largest
    unsolved problem of classical physics. Engineering copes with a hierarchy: exact
    solutions (Poiseuille laminar pipe flow: Δp = 32μLv/D²), logarithmic boundary-layer
    laws, and correlations (Moody) — and, since the 1970s, computational fluid dynamics
    (CFD), which numerically simulates flows well enough to shape every modern car
    (drag coefficients fell from ~0.5 on 1970s boxes to ~0.20 on the best today, worth
    roughly 15% of fuel) and every building's wind environment. CFD's honesty note: it
    models turbulence statistically (RANS) at industrial cost, and the models are tuned
    against experiment — wind tunnels never left.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Drag is a two-front war.</strong> Pressure (form) drag comes from the wake —
    separation of the boundary layer — and is fought by streamlining; skin-friction drag
    comes from the boundary layer itself and is fought with smoothness, laminar-flow
    surfaces, and riblets (sharkskin films flew on airliners and America's Cup hulls).
    The golf ball inverts the logic: dimples <em>trip</em> the boundary layer turbulent,
    and a turbulent layer resists separation — the wake shrinks, pressure drag halves,
    the ball flies far farther than a smooth sphere. Drag reduction, like most of this
    subject, is knowing which regime you are in and paying the regime's price.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open problems.</strong> Physiological fluid mechanics (aneurysm growth,
    stent haemodynamics, artificial heart valves) runs these same equations against
    elastic, pulsatile, non-Newtonian constraints — and still cannot fully predict which
    aneurysm will rupture. Microfluidics (lab-on-chip) operates at Re so low that
    turbulence never arrives: mixing must be engineered by diffusion and chaos-by-geometry.
    And the grand practical question — turbulence modelling good enough to replace every
    wind tunnel — remains open, with progress measured in per cent of drag, the field's
    native currency.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> White, Fluid Mechanics; Munson, Young &amp; Okiishi, Fundamentals of Fluid Mechanics; Çengel &amp; Cimbala; the physics ladder's fluid-dynamics lesson (Bernoulli, Navier–Stokes frontier).</p>

